Description: ffmpeg: backport Plex's canThrottle handler for per-packet sloth mode
 Plex's ffmpeg fork adds a PMS feedback loop inside fftools/ffmpeg.c:
 every progress block also fires a one-shot HTTP PUT and parses the
 response body for the literal substring `canThrottle`. When present,
 ffmpeg's input-packet read loop applies `usleep(100 ms)` per packet
 (~28% throughput in equilibrium) — PMS uses this to cap the transcode
 buffer at TranscoderThrottleBuffer.

 scaleplex's worker emulates this externally via SIGSTOP/SIGCONT pulses
 on the ffmpeg pgid (`worker/agent/throttle.go`). The external pulse
 has high variance — when paused, ffmpeg writes nothing for hundreds of
 milliseconds, easy to undershoot a slow-buffering client's playout
 (LG webOS observed 2026-05-11). Plex's internal usleep is constant-
 cadence and self-balancing because PMS keeps re-asserting / clearing
 canThrottle at equilibrium.

 Implementation (adapted from Plex's fftools/ffmpeg.c lines 1846 + 4651
 — the official GPL tarball drops fftools entirely, canonical reference
 is Diagonactic/plex-new-transcoder which auto-mirrors Plex's full
 source):

  - New option `-canthrottleurl <url>`. Worker rewriter injects this
    pointing at the same relay endpoint as -progressurl. Setting it
    leaves -progress / -progressurl untouched so the worker's progress
    pipe (canThrottle extraction, metrics, checkpoint) keeps working
    in parallel — both consume the response body, the per-packet sleep
    just becomes additional from ffmpeg's side.

  - print_report fires a one-shot HTTP PUT to the URL after each
    progress block (sub-second cadence per `stats_period`), reads the
    response body via the same AVIOContext, sets a global atomic
    throttle_delay_us based on whether the body contains `canThrottle`.

  - encoder_thread (fftools/ffmpeg_enc.c) sleeps once per encoded
    VIDEO output frame when the atomic is set. Picking the gate
    location requires care: Plex's 6.x base ran a single-threaded
    transcode loop, so their sleep after process_input_packet was
    naturally rate-limited by encoder pace (one sleep per loop iter,
    one loop iter per fully-processed input packet). Jellyfin 7.x split
    demuxer and encoder into separate threads connected by a bounded
    queue; sleeping in the demuxer thread (our 2026-05-11 first cut)
    fires at queue-drain rate, which is ~1.5× faster than encoder
    consumption for 4K HEVC HDR pass-through, choking throughput to
    1.17× realtime when canThrottle is asserted vs Plex's stated ~28%
    floor (which equilibrates near realtime because canThrottle
    oscillates as PMS sees the buffer drain). Putting the sleep in the
    video encoder thread, after frame_encode returns, restores the
    encoder-paced cadence: 23.976 fps × 100 ms ≈ 2.4 s sleep per
    wall-second when asserted ≈ 28% throughput, exactly the Plex
    documented rate. Audio + subtitle encoder threads run in parallel
    and MUST NOT sleep — they'd compound the throttle multiplicatively.

Origin: backport, https://github.com/Diagonactic/plex-new-transcoder
        (Plex Transcoder 1.12.3.4973), fftools/ffmpeg.c L1846-L1862
        and L4649-L4653.
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: scaleplex
Last-Update: 2026-05-12
